  {
    "episode_id": 94413,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/4/94413.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 6,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, well, I think it's unfortunate that conservation has gotten so politicized that somehow if you are conservative, if you're Republican, you can't be pro-environment, pro-out doors, pro-public lands. Because I am. And as you mentioned, one of the great compliments I had was a headline that called me the conservative conservationist. And I think about what we have in Montana is that if you don't have a balance on those views here as it relates to allowing us to respond to develop our natural resources, we must continue to do that for a lot of reasons, but one is to have an economy in jobs because if we don't have a natural resource economy placed like Montana, then ultimately it's just the kind of the rich and famous that we will to live in a place like Montana and the average working men and women will have to leave because they don't have a job. As we've seen some of these radical environmental groups shut down mining, shut down timber management, shut down oil, gas in our coal industries. Well, what do you have if you shut all those down? You basically just have a state that only people who are wealthy can't afford to live in.",
