    "turn_text": "And I'll tell you, you've got to be very, very careful. And I take all the precautions. I have bear spray as well as a firearm when I'm out in grizzly country. But you're exactly right. The science, the science is telling us Just quantitative, I'm a chemical engineer by degree, Gabriel, I like to look at the numbers from the science. The science tells us the bear now has exceeded the recovery targets. Let's celebrate that. We've actually recovered the species and indeed your species act. And let's de-list them, move the management species back to Montana. Like we did with wolves, that's working very well by the way. Let Montana manage a predator like the wolf. But it's in the best interest of the bears. It's in the best interest of for the people. It's the best interest for agriculture. As you mentioned, predator losses. The number one loss in livestock now in Montana is grizzly bears. It's not wolves, it's not mountain lions. It's grizzly bears. We have families up along the Rocky Mountain front there along the Bob Marshall Center there. where they have to walk their children to the bus stop. They're armed in the morning because there's so many grizzly bears now that remember when Lewis and Clark came out to Montana, the grizzly bears were on the plains. Grizzly bears are omnivores. They eat primarily grass and berries and so forth. They also eat meat, but let's not forget the fact we now have grizzly bears out in the wheat fields of Montana in places like Conrad and outside of Great Falls. And so We need to de-list the bear, return to manage the species back to the people of Montana.",
